{
  "chalk": 1,
  "name": "02-matrices.chalk",
  "cells": [
    {
      "src": "Matrices",
      "type": "section",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "> [!goal]\n> Multiply matrices, see that the order matters, transpose, and apply a matrix to a vector.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "Rows are separated by `;`: `[1, 2; 3, 4]` is the $2 \\times 2$ matrix with first row $1, 2$.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "let A = [1, 2; 3, 4]",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "let B = [0, 1; 1, 0]",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "> [!definition] Matrix product\n> The entry in row $i$, column $j$ of $AB$ is the dot product of row $i$ of $A$ with column $j$ of $B$.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "A*B",
      "showWork": true,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "> [!mistake]\n> Matrix multiplication is not commutative: $AB$ and $BA$ are different matrices. Here $B$ swaps: on the right of $A$ it swaps $A$'s columns, on the left its rows.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "B*A",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "transpose(A)",
      "showWork": true,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "A matrix acting on a vector",
      "type": "section",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "A column vector is a matrix with one column, `[1; 1]`. $A$ sends it to the sum of $A$'s columns.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "A*[1; 1]",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "[1, 2; 3, 4]*[0, 1; 1, 0]",
      "type": "exercise",
      "prompt": "Multiply.",
      "hints": [
        "Row $i$ of the first times column $j$ of the second.",
        "The answer is a matrix: type it as `[a, b; c, d]`."
      ],
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "[2, 0; 1, 3]*[1; 2]",
      "type": "exercise",
      "prompt": "Apply the matrix to the vector.",
      "hints": [
        "The first entry is $2 \\cdot 1 + 0 \\cdot 2$.",
        "The answer is a column: `[a; b]`."
      ],
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "transpose([1, 2, 3; 4, 5, 6])",
      "type": "exercise",
      "prompt": "Transpose the $2 \\times 3$ matrix.",
      "hints": [
        "Rows become columns: the answer is $3 \\times 2$."
      ],
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "> [!summary]\n> A matrix product is a table of dot products, and its order matters; a matrix times a vector is a combination of the matrix's columns.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    }
  ],
  "scenes": []
}
